The call-site census is complete: seven sites, split into four GETs and three PUTs. Two functions produce the mode arguments. The callee does clamp some device accesses, but ordinary RAM writes preserve the full requested length. Also, a YCRCB-only check covers 1/12 of the default-arm encodings, or 1/16 of all color-type encodings.
codex
**VERDICT: Claim 1 is correct: 12 of 16 color-type encodings reach the default arm. Claim 2 is also supported by the source: guest-driven PUT paths can copy uninitialised bytes into guest RAM, including implemented color modes.**

I inspected `GXEMUL-SEC/src/` only. These are source-derived findings; actual leaked contents remain unmeasured.

I examined **all seven call sites in two caller functions: four GETs and three PUTs**. All device references below refer to `GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c`.

| Caller and loop | GET call lines | PUT call lines |
|---|---:|---:|
| [`draw_primitive()`, line loop](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:809) | 817 | 863 |
| [`draw_primitive()`, nested rectangle loops](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:871) | 878, 883 | 929 |
| [`do_mte_transfer()`, nested tiled-transfer loops](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1392) | 1397 | 1402 |

For mode provenance, I examined **both argument-producing functions**, **two direct register-writer functions containing six store statements**, and **one device initializer**:

- **`dev_sgi_de_access()`** writes `de_reg`: two stores for an eight-byte access, one for a four-byte access, at lines 1003, 1004 and 1010. These preserve the relevant mode bits. The `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC` and `CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` switch arms only print diagnostics. [`draw_primitive()` loads both registers unchanged](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:702) and never modifies those local modes. [Writer and register handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:982).
- **`dev_sgi_mte_access()`** writes `mte_reg` through three stores, at lines 1539, 1544 and 1545; its mode-register arm also only prints. [`do_mte_transfer()` constructs the two helper arguments](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1365) from the MTE addressing and depth fields, then conditionally adds RGBA. [MTE writer](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1516).
- **[`dev_sgi_re_init()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:675)** zeroes the device structure. That initialization does not cover the helper’s separate automatic `buf`.

Claim 1 follows from the **unrestricted DE path**, not merely from arithmetic on the quoted switch. The [header’s mask](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h:328) admits sixteen encodings; [`horrible_getputpixel()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:284) handles four explicitly. The other twelve—`0x40` through `0xf0` in increments of `0x10`—select `default:`. Nothing in the DE writer or `draw_primitive()` restricts these to the five named types.

There **are earlier returns** in [`horrible_getputpixel()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:196): coordinate bounds, tile-index bounds, unsupported addressing modes and the tile-pointer validity check. These restrict whether a pixel operation proceeds; they do not validate the color-type field. The twelve-count applies when those addressing checks pass.

One denominator correction: a YCRCB-only check covers **1/12 of the default-arm encodings**, and **1/16 of the entire color-type field**.

For claim 2, **eight-byte depth is reachable, and CI is not restricted to one-byte depth**. The DE writer preserves bits 9:8, and the helper evaluates `1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3)` without rejecting the fourth encoding. The [header names only three buffer-depth encodings](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h:325); that naming imposes no runtime restriction.

The MTE path independently confirms that these mismatches survive actual argument construction. In [`do_mte_transfer()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1300), the depth field produces 8/16/32/64 **bits**, and is carried into the helper’s byte-depth field. The current code adds RGBA **only when `depth == 32`**:

| MTE depth in bits | Helper color type | Helper `bufdepth` |
|---:|---|---:|
| 8 | CI | 1 |
| 16 | CI | 2 |
| 32 | RGBA | 4 |
| 64 | CI | 8 |

This follows from the live [mode-construction and conditional assignments](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1365), independently of the `#509` comment. Its 32-bit repair is present. The later depth rejection belongs to the **linear-MTE branch**, not the tiled branch calling this helper.

Consequently, the PUT initialization census in [`horrible_getputpixel()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:284) is exactly:

| PUT arm | Bytes assigned | Depths transmitting unassigned bytes |
|---|---:|---|
| CI | 1 | 2, 4, 8: respectively **1, 3, 7** unassigned bytes |
| RGB / RGBA / ABGR | 4 | 8: **4** unassigned bytes |
| Default | 4 | 8: **4** unassigned bytes |

The callee **does copy the requested length on an ordinary RAM write**. [`mips_cpu_new()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_mips.c:110) installs `mips_memory_rw`; [the generated MIPS inclusion](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/tmp_mips_tail.c:38) instantiates `cpus/memory_rw.c`.

In that implementation:

- [`mips_memory_rw()` and its worker](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:183) bypass virtual translation for `PHYSICAL` and retain `len`.
- The worker’s RAM write is literally [`memcpy(memblock + offset, data, len)`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:779). Crossing a memory-block boundary splits the transfer and forwards the remaining bytes; it does not truncate the total.
- There **is device-boundary clamping**, and `NO_EXCEPTIONS` can suppress device-handler calls. Those are [MMIO-dispatch behaviors](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:480), not padding or sanitization of a RAM-write source buffer. The nearby zero-fill operations apply to reads.

Guest reachability also survives the caller audit. [`dev_sgi_de_access()` invokes `draw_primitive()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1267), and [`dev_sgi_mte_access()` invokes `do_mte_transfer()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1639). [`dev_sgi_re_access()` accepts guest-written tile mappings](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:428). These are registered device paths, with normal addressing preconditions; no firmware-only assignment set restricts the modes.

Your `fatal()` premise is correct too. [`fatal()` in `core/debugmsg.c`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:384) calls the returning [`va_debug()` printer](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:118). The helper’s color-default diagnostic has no latch or verbosity guard, and execution continues into the memory write.

Therefore **the DE line and rectangle loops can produce a guest-driven per-pixel output flood**. The GET color-default arm also prints on every invocation. The earlier `SGIRE_W_BADMODE` latch guards the addressing-mode diagnostic only. Although MTE also calls the helper per pixel, its current CI/RGBA construction does **not** reach the color-default diagnostic.

I found an additional issue in the GET half: **it can consume more bytes than it reads**. [`horrible_getputpixel()` GET](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:319) requests `bufdepth` bytes, but RGB consumes `buf[0..2]`, while RGBA, ABGR and default consume `buf[0..3]`. At depths one or two, those arms use uninitialised trailing bytes. CI GET has no such mismatch. The source GET result feeds the later PUT in `draw_primitive()`’s rectangle loop, so this can also propagate into guest memory.

The current RGB GET expression does incorporate the `#505` layout correction. That correction leaves the GET length mismatch intact.
